Linux Basics Quick Overview of the last two days 

🔹 Users & Groups
Q: How do you create a new user with home directory & shell?
 A: sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash user && sudo passwd user

Q: File storing user info vs. passwords?
 A: /etc/passwd (users), /etc/shadow (encrypted passwords).

Q: Switch user without logging out?
 A: su - user or sudo -u user -i

Q: Check groups of a user?
 A: id user or groups user

Q: Lock/Unlock account?
 A: usermod -L user / usermod -U user

Q: Difference between su and sudo?
 A: su → switch user (needs target password), sudo → run as root (your password).


🔹 Permissions
Q: What does -rwxr-xr-- mean?
 A: Owner rwx, group r-x, others r--.

Q: Numeric vs symbolic permissions?
 A: Numeric → chmod 755 file; Symbolic → chmod u=rwx,g=rx,o=r file.

Q: Change file ownership?
 A: chown user:group file

Q: SUID, SGID, Sticky Bit?
 A: SUID → run as owner, SGID → inherit group, Sticky Bit → only owner can delete (e.g. /tmp).

Q: Give execute permission only to owner?
 A: chmod u+x file


🔹 Services & Processes
Q: Check if service is running?
 A: systemctl status sshd

Q: Enable service at boot?
 A: systemctl enable nginx

Q: Restart a service?
 A: systemctl restart nginx

Q: Show all running processes?
 A: ps aux or top

Q: Find process using port 80?
 A: lsof -i :80 or ss -tulpn | grep 80

Q: Difference between kill, pkill, killall?
 A: kill PID, pkill name, killall name.


🔹 Logs & Monitoring
Q: Where are Linux logs stored?
 A: /var/log/

Q: Auth logs location?
 A: /var/log/auth.log (Debian), /var/log/secure (RHEL).

Q: View logs in real-time?
 A: tail -f /var/log/syslog or journalctl -f

Q: Kernel logs?
 A: dmesg or journalctl -k

Q: Show last 50 log lines?
 A: tail -n 50 file.log

Q: Clear a log file?
 A: truncate -s 0 file.log

🔹 Scenario-Based
Q: User can’t log in → first checks?
 A: passwd -S user, /var/log/auth.log, shell in /etc/passwd, disk space df -h.

Q: Service running but port not responding?
 A: Check systemctl status, ss -tulpn, firewall, journalctl -u service.

Q: Fix “Permission denied” on directory?
 A: ls -ld dir, check owner, perms, ACLs.

Q: CPU at 100% → how to find culprit?
 A: top or ps -eo pid,ppid,cmd,%mem,%cpu --sort=-%cpu | head.

Q: Disk full → what do you check?
 A: df -h, du -sh *, clear /var/log/, old files.
